  "notes": [
    "cycleType 'week-of-year-recurring' was added to this schema specifically for this file, per Josh's direct decision 2026-09-30 (\"a different prayer every week for a year... they will pray that all week\") -- see schema.json's own `rules` for the full record. Each entry holds ONE week's intention for the entire week, keyed by a bare week number with no calendar date or year printed anywhere in the source.",
    "The roster's own pre-fetch research note described this as a \"51-week calendar\", but the live page (fetched 2026-09-30) actually runs Week 1 through Week 52 -- transcribed as currently published, not truncated or padded to match the earlier research note.",
    "Weeks 1-13 and 51-52 name dioceses, provinces, committees, schools, and other non-congregation subjects (recorded as `category`); weeks 14-50 each name one or more specific parishes by \"TOWN: Parish Name\" (recorded as `parish`, place = the town exactly as printed, always in the source's own all-caps style -- not normalized to title case).",
    "Two entries (weeks 23 and 40-41) print a comma instead of the colon used to separate town from parish name everywhere else in the source; both are disclosed on the affected subject's own `note` rather than silently reformatted to match.",
    "Week 24 (\"MCCOMB/MAGNOLIA: Mediator/Redeemer\") compresses two towns and two parish short-names into one slash-paired line, unlike every other multi-parish week's plain semicolon-separated list. Split into two subjects pairing town-to-name by position, disclosed as a judgment call on both affected subjects.",
    "Week 31 (\"JACKSON: St. Mark's; JACKSON: St. Christopher's Church of the Ascension; JACKSON: St. James'; JACKSON: St. Philip's\") appears to be missing a semicolon between two distinct Jackson parishes (\"St. Christopher's\" and a separate \"Church of the Ascension\", the latter also independently named in Hattiesburg in week 26) -- split into two subjects, disclosed as a likely source punctuation drop.",
    "The source's own possessive apostrophes are inconsistent throughout -- most \"Name's\" forms use a curly right single quote (e.g. \"St. Mark's\"), while every \"Names'\" form ending the word itself in the apostrophe (e.g. \"All Saints'\", \"St. James'\", \"St. Francis'\", \"St. Thomas'\") uses a plain straight apostrophe instead. This reads as a smart-quotes artifact of the diocese's own CMS (which correctly curls an apostrophe followed by a letter but leaves a trailing, letter-less apostrophe straight) rather than a deliberate style choice; both forms are preserved exactly as printed throughout, not normalized to match each other.",
    "\"Church of the Redeemer\" is spelled out in full for Ocean Springs/Biloxi (week 16), Brookhaven (week 24), and Greenville (week 39), while the same dedication is printed as the bare short form \"Redeemer\" for Magnolia (week 24) -- both forms preserved exactly as printed on their own entries, not harmonized.",
    "Fetched directly via curl with a standard browser User-Agent; the page's content loads as plain server-rendered HTML (a numbered list, not a table), confirmed via BeautifulSoup parsing of the raw markup rather than a linearized text extraction, so none of this corpus's usual PDF column-interleaving risk applies here."
  ],
